===Alternative forms===
See [[#Usage notes|Usage notes]]

===Phrase===
'''Lord willing and the [[creek]] don't rise'''

# {{idiom|US|informal}} Barring unforeseen circumstances.
#: '''''Lord willing and the creek don't rise''', we'll have that new barn finished in time for the harvest.''

====Usage notes====
* Numerous alternative forms can be found, some examples of which are shown on the Talk page. 
**For "Lord" common substitutes are "Good Lord" and "God".
**For "creek", "crick" and "river" are common.
**The weather is often a substitute for the the second part of the phrase
**The devil is invoked as a source of trouble.
**There are numerous substitutions of worldly agency, sometimes for humorous effect.

{{fact}}
The phrase originated with Benjamin Hawkins, Creek Indian agent around the time of the War of 1812.  It  should be correctly written as 'God willing and the Creek don't rise'. Hawkins, college-educated and a well-written man would never have made a grammatical error, so the capitalization of Creek is the only way the phrase could make sense. He wrote it in response to a request from the President to return to our Nation's Capital and the reference is not to a creek, but The Creek Indian Nation. If the Creek "rose," Hawkins would have to be present to quell the rebellion

